--TEST--
The one construct the front end accepts and nothing below it compiles yet is refused at its own site — `spawn script` is `E0703` (ADR 0006, M5) — while `require` is whole in both of ADR 0021's forms and reports nothing at either
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0021 § 3's value form runs now: the site calls the target file's own
// script frame and keeps what its file-scope `return` handed back, typed
// `mixed` because it cannot be known statically. `nvs-ir`'s known gap 22 is
// closed and `E0704` is retired with it — no diagnostic below names this
// line, which is what this case still asserts about it.
mixed $c = require "cfg.nvs";
echo $c as string;

// The statement form is the same call with its value discarded, and the
// class the target declares is reachable from here either way: declarations
// cross a `require` (§ *Decision*).
require "cfg.nvs";
echo Cfg::name();

// ADR 0006's isolate spawn, which nothing below the checker compiles yet
// (`docs/plan/m5.md`). Refusing it where it is written is the only
// reading that cannot silently do nothing.
spawn script "cfg.nvs";
--FILE cfg.nvs--
<?nvs
class Cfg {
    public static function name(): string {
        return "cfg";
    }
}
return "cfg-value";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0703]: `spawn script` is not compiled yet
   --> %s:19:1
   |
19 | spawn script "cfg.nvs";
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no isolate is created here
   = help: ADR 0006's isolates arrive with `docs/plan/m5.md`, together with the value-crossing copy a spawn boundary needs. There is no same-frame spelling of this to fall back on

